From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PS3 Linux project <ps3@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0300
Subject: ps3: System Manager temperature sensors as hwmon

SYSCON measures the Cell BE and RSX temperatures (and, on some boards,
more thermal zones), and GameOS reads them through the System Manager
with sys_game_get_temperature (lv2 syscall 383, service id 13). Linux
had no way to see them.

With CONFIG_PS3_SYS_MANAGER_HWMON the sys-manager driver registers a
hwmon device "ps3_sm" with temp*_input, temp*_label and temp*_alarm
for the zones in ps3_sys_manager.temp_zones (default 0,1: Cell BE,
RSX). Reading temp*_input sends one temperature request on the
driver's own vuart port and waits up to a second for the reply, which
the existing message handler picks up; values are cached for a second
and one request is outstanding at a time. The bytes are those
the get_temperature command of ps3vuart-tools (glevand, sguerrini97)
sends through /dev/ps3sysmngr under OtherOS++: a header with tag 0 and
payload_size 4 (the tool's struct is padded to 20 bytes), payload
{ version 1, tzone, 0, 0 }; the reply payload is { version, tzone,
status, 0, integer degrees, 1/256 degrees, 0 }. The reply's service
id and size are not documented: service 13 or 14 with 1-16 payload
bytes is taken, and the first one seen is logged.

Nothing but that get request is sent. A REQUEST_ERROR reply, or three
failed requests in a row, turns the sensors off for the boot, so a
System Manager that doesn't offer the service to the Linux LPAR isn't
asked again; a reply with a non-zero status switches off that zone
only. The driver's shutdown hook (device_shutdown, before power off,
reboot and kexec) takes the request mutex and stops requests, so none
is in flight when the final messages go out, and the final power-off
and restart paths stop them too (the panic path skips device
shutdown). While /dev/ps3sysmngr is open (it reads the port itself)
the sensors return -EBUSY. A temperature reply returns non-zero from
the message handler so ps3_sys_manager_fin() keeps waiting for the
shutdown command. temp*_alarm follows the THERMAL_ALERT/CLEARED
events the driver already receives, without any request. Unbinding
the driver through sysfs is off (remove was never supported).

Changes to the existing paths, so that a second sender and an
unexpected reply can't break the message stream:
- ps3_sys_manager_write() sends header and payload in one vuart write,
  so a temperature request from another thread can't land between
  them.
- send_attr's payload is 16 bytes, as its payload_size always said;
  the write sent 8 bytes of stack after the attribute, now zeros.
- A header with an unexpected payload size no longer BUG()s: it is
  logged and the queued bytes are dropped.
- An unhandled message's payload, and everything queued after a bad
  header, is read out of the rx list; ps3_vuart_clear_rx_bytes()
  drains only the hypervisor's buffer and left the payload queued to
  be parsed as the next header.

ps3_sys_manager.hwmon=0 on the command line turns the sensors off.

Forwarded: not yet (test on the PS3 first)

diff --git a/arch/powerpc/platforms/ps3/Kconfig b/arch/powerpc/platforms/ps3/Kconfig
--- a/arch/powerpc/platforms/ps3/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/powerpc/platforms/ps3/Kconfig
@@ -87,6 +87,24 @@ config PS3_SYS_MANAGER
 	  This support is required for PS3 system control.  In
 	  general, all users will say Y or M.
 
+config PS3_SYS_MANAGER_HWMON
+	bool "PS3 System Manager temperature sensors (hwmon)"
+	depends on PS3_SYS_MANAGER
+	depends on HWMON=y || HWMON=PS3_SYS_MANAGER
+	help
+	  Read-only hwmon device "ps3_sm" with the temperatures SYSCON
+	  measures: the Cell BE and the RSX by default, other thermal zones
+	  with ps3_sys_manager.temp_zones=.  Reading a temp*_input sends the
+	  System Manager a temperature request (service 13, the one GameOS's
+	  sys_game_get_temperature uses) and waits for the reply; a value is
+	  cached for a second.  Nothing else is sent: no fan or SYSCON
+	  setting is touched.  If the System Manager refuses the request or
+	  doesn't answer, the sensors read as unavailable and no more
+	  requests are sent.  temp*_alarm follows the System Manager's
+	  thermal alert events.
+
+	  ps3_sys_manager.hwmon=0 on the command line turns it off.
+
 config PS3_VERBOSE_RESULT
 	bool "PS3 Verbose LV1 hypercall results" if PS3_ADVANCED
 	depends on PPC_PS3
diff --git a/drivers/ps3/ps3-sys-manager.c b/drivers/ps3/ps3-sys-manager.c
--- a/drivers/ps3/ps3-sys-manager.c
+++ b/drivers/ps3/ps3-sys-manager.c
@@ -11,6 +11,11 @@
